# Create and activate conda environment
conda create -n <env_name> -c bioconda <tool_name>
conda activate <env_name>

# Tools Installation
conda install -c bioconda nanoplot
conda install -c bioconda minimap2
conda install -c bioconda unicycler
conda install -c bioconda quast
conda install -c bioconda busco
conda install -c bioconda flye
conda install -c bioconda prokka
conda install -c bioconda genovi

For best practices, use SLURM scripts for installation on HPC systems.

##Workflow Summary

1. Quality Control
NanoPlot: Generates quality plots for short and long reads.
2. Merging Reads
Merging simplifies downstream steps like trimming, filtering, and assembly.
3. Genome Assembly
Minimap2: Aligns long reads for genome assembly.
Unicycler: Combines short and long reads for hybrid assembly.
Flye: Creates high-quality assemblies from long reads.
4. Quality Assessment
QUAST: Computes metrics like N50, genome size, and alignment statistics.
BUSCO: Evaluates genome completeness based on orthologous genes.
5. Genome Annotation
Prokka: Annotates assemblies for genomic elements like CDS, tRNAs, and rRNAs.
6. Visualization
Genovi: Creates graphical representations of annotations.

###Execution Examples

##Running NanoPlot

#!/bin/bash
#SBATCH --job-name=nanoplot_job
#SBATCH --partition=hpc
#SBATCH --nodes=1
#SBATCH --ntasks-per-node=1
#SBATCH --mem=8G
#SBATCH --time=02:00:00
#SBATCH --output=/shared/home/<username>/slurm-nanoplot-%j.out

source $HOME/.bash_profile
conda activate <path_to_conda_env>
NanoPlot --fastq <input_path> --threads 8 --plots kde dot -o <output_path>
conda deactivate

##Running QUAST

#!/bin/bash
#SBATCH --job-name=quast_job
#SBATCH --partition=hpc
#SBATCH --nodes=1
#SBATCH --ntasks-per-node=8
#SBATCH --mem=30G
#SBATCH --time=24:00:00
#SBATCH --output=/shared/home/<username>/slurm-quast-%j.out

source $HOME/.bash_profile
conda activate <path_to_conda_env>
python /shared/conda/shared/bin/quast -o <output_path> -r <reference_genome> -g <gff_file> <assembly_file>
conda deactivate

##Running Prokka

#!/bin/bash
#SBATCH --job-name=prokka_job
#SBATCH --partition=hpc
#SBATCH --nodes=1
#SBATCH --ntasks-per-node=8
#SBATCH --mem=30G
#SBATCH --time=24:00:00
#SBATCH --output=/shared/home/<username>/slurm-prokka-%j.out

source $HOME/.bash_profile
conda activate <path_to_conda_env>
prokka --outdir <output_path> --prefix <prefix_name> --kingdom <kingdom> --genus <genus> <assembly_file>
conda deactivate

